WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Donkey Kong Classics!

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.520
What's more fun than a barrel of monkeys?

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.080
A Donkey Kong Double Feature!

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:30.520
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:31.580
I'm Mike.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.160
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:40.920
And guys, today we are talking about DKC, not Donkey Kong Country, Donkey Kong Classics.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.680
For the first time ever, Donkey Kong and DK Jr.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.320
together. Except for they were together in Donkey Kong Jr.,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:57.740
but one of them was in jail because of Mario or Jumpman, or actually his name was Mario in that one, not Jumpman like it was in Donkey Kong.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:03.540
And Jumpman and Mario were in both of them, and now it's just the same games put on Donkey Kong Classics.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:04.460
You guys get the idea, right?

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.660
Yeah, it's easy to follow.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.820
So what you're saying is that there are two games on this cartridge.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.620
Two games in one, Sean.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.060
That's insane.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:14.520
For the same value.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:22.580
Yeah. So, you know, I don't know about you guys, but, you know, I remember Donkey Kong and Donkey Kong Jr.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:23.740
both pretty well,

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:29.300
and when I started up this game, I was kind of surprised to see that the menu had changed.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.060
Yeah, the menu is very different now.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.760
And maybe we could just, like, talk about that.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:40.960
Well, it's got one of the, like, the classic color gamuts here.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:52.960
Like, you've got sort of like a dark blue, or like a deep blue, and a sort of pinkish red for the title itself.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:02:00.540
And you've got black and white outline, and then white text, and like a lighter blue for some less important text.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:04.260
So I think it's a really good...

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.560
It's a really good palette to work with.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:13.640
Now, do you think that that light blue with the dark blue is a solid blue dark background,

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:20.580
and then the light blue has, like, the copyright information for Nintendo, and the game that's not currently selected?

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.920
Do you think that that's just a little too close in color for each other?

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.680
I think that the color is fine.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.660
I think what it says politically, though, is wrong.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.080
I think that it's actually...

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.980
I feel like it's deliberately close in color, because it's like the game that you don't have selected.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.740
So it's supposed to sort of, like, blend into the background a little more.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.920
You don't know... It's not supposed to be as prominent as the one that you do have selected.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.980
Right. And, you know, this kind of...

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.400
This is going to get me into a big rant, but I feel like we need to talk about this.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:01.780
Is that in the original black box NES games, first off, there were never solid blue backgrounds.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.380
I don't know why Nintendo all of a sudden got crazy here and made a colored background.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:06.740
They were always solid black.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.720
That's why we call them the black box games, right?

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:11.460
Indeed. Yeah.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.360
We actually call them the black box games because the boxes are black, Sean.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:21.960
That was a test. But, on top of that, you would hit the select button to change modes in those.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.880
Because they always had, like, one player game A, one player game B, two player game A, etc.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.380
You know? So you would hit the select button to change between those modes.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.500
However, in this menu, you just hit the up and down arrows to select your mode.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.780
And you hit the select button to change which game you want to be playing.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:49.480
Now, what really makes that troublesome for someone like me is that I never understood the function of the select button anyway.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.980
You weren't selecting your choice.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.020
That's what the start button is for.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:00.800
You see, the select button is more like an options button to shuffle through the options that are available to you.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:06.740
So when you go from game A to game B, when you hit select, you're really not saying, I want to play game A.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.880
You're saying, I would like to select the next option.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.900
So why do you think the NES controller has a select button?

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.360
Well, I think it's weird just that they're using it in...

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.160
I know it's weird that they've used it at all.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.380
But it's weird that they're using it in this.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.440
If they're using up and down to go up and down, why aren't they using left and right to go left and right?

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.160
Joe, that's blowing my mind.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.300
And I feel like that's why, you know, maybe a lot of people didn't buy this game because there's too much of a barrier to entry.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.440
Like, you have to be a computer whiz to figure out how to operate it.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:42.620
Well, to be fair, they put that in the manual, you know, like they do explain how to choose all this stuff there.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.260
So thankfully, as long as you can read...

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.360
Nobody wants to read that thing.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.000
Joe, I just tested the game.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.440
And if you press left or right on the main menu, it does go between Donkey Kong and Donkey Kong Jr.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:56.320
Oh, it does. So the select button...

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.200
It does do left and right.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:58.940
Does the select button also do that?

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:00.060
It very well may.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.140
I did not test that.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.220
I just wanted to make sure I could tell if you were wrong.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:04.800
Great. Well, I appreciate that.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.860
I appreciate that. Well, I was only going off of what Mike had just said, that select switches between Donkey Kong and Donkey Kong Jr.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.980
And then up and down switches between game A and game B.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.120
And you know what?

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.760
I also just checked and the select button does do that as well.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.340
But I wanted to make sure that we were 100% correct.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:30.820
But if you ask me, like, why this system has a select button, I don't know.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:34.940
Because I usually see the position that that button is placed.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.080
That button is only for sharing gameplay footage.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:45.340
And I guess, like, they just decided that they didn't want to do that with the NES and maybe this game specifically.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:53.440
But every other game that I've played, that button is just for taking screenshots and streaming on Twitch.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:55.200
That's true.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:10.120
And, you know, another thing that I'm noticing now that I look at this menu screen a little more is that Donkey Kong, the O's in Donkey Kong have the stars from the arcade cabinet logo for Donkey Kong.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.040
And that's pretty nice.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.960
I always thought that was a little presumptuous.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.700
Like, you know, I didn't play this on the arcade cabinet.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.240
So why is it trying to force that aesthetic choice on me?

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.640
Well, here's my theory on that.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:33.720
If you go back to the original Donkey Kong for the NES menu screen, there's no star in the actual title.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.940
But the cursor that indicates which game mode you've selected is an asterisk.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.700
Okay, so I think when Donkey Kong Jr.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:47.020
came out, they brought in a new asterisk, a yellow one instead of a blue one.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:54.960
So they retired the original asterisk and put a star in the junior to represent that original asterisk.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:04.800
And now I think in this one, there are two stars, one representing the original Donkey Kong, one representing Donkey Kong Jr., and then a new asterisk representing this union.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:11.220
Yeah, that's actually a very insightful look at that.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:22.520
But to me, like, whatever symbolism and allegory it has in its typography, all I can see is the O's look too much like A's and it looks like it says Donkey Kong classics.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:30.200
I would agree with that if there were any other O's on this screen that didn't have the star.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.140
I would agree that that would be a little confusing.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:36.000
Thankfully, the only O's on the screen also have the stars.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:44.240
And there's an A in classics that shows us the difference between, like, what an A in this typeface looks like versus an O.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.780
Right, I agree if you're thinking about it that much.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:50.080
But to Sean's point, it could also be another, like, barrier to entry.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:55.320
People don't know what game they're playing because they don't even get to the word classics.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:59.200
By the time they read Donkey Kong, they're not sure what those letters are and they turn it off.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.260
I'm already out.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.120
Like, they've lost me once I've read those first two words.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:12.600
Really, anyone who doesn't have a law degree can't play this game because there's so many trademarks and reserves and copyrights.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.780
It's true. It's intimidating.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.920
I think this is also the end user license agreement, like, for the game.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:24.560
Well, because I also wonder, like, when I look at the screen, like, will I go to jail if I play this game?

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.420
Like, I don't, it's a registered trademark.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:33.500
Can I play it? You can't actually display these things on your television without the express written consent of Nintendo.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.640
Gotcha. They usually say stuff like that in the, on the manual anyway, at the end.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.960
And there's, like, a whole thing, a final page of, like, don't put this on a chair.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.240
Don't leave this on for more than 50 minutes.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:51.120
There's a whole set of, like, rules that you legally agree to when you buy these games.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.920
I forgot about not leaving it on chairs.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.580
Well, because someone could sit on it.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:59.120
It's true. Exactly.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:07.480
Well, one nice thing about this menu, though, is that, so there is a box or a frame around Donkey Kong classics.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.520
And it would have been really easy for them to just make a rectangular box.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:13.840
Like, fine, easy.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.520
No one probably would have complained.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:16.820
But they really went.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:18.700
They got those divots. Yeah, they went the extra mile.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:27.440
And since Donkey Kong, those two words together, are longer than the word classics, they made a longer portion of the box to fit Donkey Kong than classics.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.220
Which really just shows, like, how much care went into this game.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:39.020
Because I know anyone who's used Microsoft Paint or Photoshop or anything knows that it's really easy to just grab that rectangle tool and make a perfect regular rectangle.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.560
And if you didn't want to, like, put in the extra work, you just leave at that.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.700
Sure, Joe. I mean, it's true.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:47.260
This is better than just a rectangle.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:53.820
But from my point of view, they shouldn't have been boxing this in any way.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:57.460
You know what? Be a man and make a drop shadow.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.180
You know? It doesn't have to have a box surrounding the letters.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.440
Just let the letters stand with a drop shadow.

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.660
Yeah, it is hard to see the letters a little bit with that red against blue.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:10.760
Yeah. I mean, again, it's one of the classic color palettes of our time.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:12.820
But you're right, it does make it hard to see.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.920
Do you guys think they're assuming too much here, though, by having Donkey Kong be selected first?

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:25.120
Like, automatically, if you don't know that you can select Donkey Kong Jr., you might think that you only bought Donkey Kong.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.500
Right. And I have no idea how you select Donkey Kong Jr.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:29.840
math. It's not even clear.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.020
It's not in the manual. You know what?

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.480
That's something I wanted to get into.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:40.300
When it's Donkey Kong classics, and there's no Donkey Kong 3, and there's no Donkey Kong Jr.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:43.740
math, this is an incomplete collection.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.740
No Donkey Kong country. Or they're saying those aren't the classics.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:55.020
They very well may be saying that, but who gave them the right to say what is and is not a Donkey Kong classic?

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:58.200
The fans should have to say.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:10:59.500
Should have their say.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:03.400
You know, this is just a minor nitpick thing, but it's bothering me now.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:13.920
Did you guys notice that one player, one player, two player, two player, the way it's structured on game A, game B, game A, game B, those are not, those are like one word.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:24.520
Whereas game A and one player are all separately spaced with appropriate spacing, one player does not have a space between one and the P in player.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.420
Yeah, that is interesting. I actually haven't noticed that, but I'll never unsee that now.

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:44.900
Yeah, I don't like that, but I think the biggest crime, and while it may be like this in other games, I still think it should be one player game A, and then two player game A, and then one player game B, and two player game B.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:50.480
I think it should be separated by the game type, and not by how many players.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:52.880
And why did they just stop at A and B?

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:54.340
They could have went all the way to Z.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:56.300
Right, right.

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.180
And it's like they could have just done...

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:07.100
A, B, C, and D, and then you have to figure out for yourself, you know, make it a little more of a challenge for the player to figure out if it's going to be one player or two player and which game it's going to be.

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:13.240
Or there could be no menu at all and would just randomly drop you into any assortment of games.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:14.780
Oh, I like that.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:17.680
Kind of like a, like Togepi's metronome move.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.800
From Pokemon, for those of you that don't know.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.480
Everyone knows. You know, I don't want to harp on the menu for too much longer,

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:31.680
but just looking at the Donkey Kong Classics typeface again, since Sean really opened my eyes to this.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:42.900
You know, does anyone else think that the L on Classics is just a little, little thin and longer than all of the other spacings on any of the other letters?

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:46.380
I didn't notice the thin, but I definitely think it's like taller.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:47.480
No, no, no.

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:53.160
It's not taller, but it's, it's the, the bottom part of the L is actually longer than the rest of them.

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:54.400
It takes up too much space.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.080
It's terrible kerning. Kerning.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:57.760
That's the word I was looking for.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:04.440
So honestly, this is, you know, this, this is the best of times, the worst of times on this menu right now.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:05.320
Well, I think though.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:08.940
Because I can't get over, the colors are just so great though.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:18.760
But the one thing that we're all missing here is that if speaking of the colors, the background color, if you look at the negative space between the E and the Y, it's a backwards F.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:26.560
So is that telling you that like this game is going to be like an F grade or is it going to be like fantastic?

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:28.440
Maybe it's saying that this game is fantastic.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:40.580
And, and how Japanese of them to just in, you know, the way they're centering the font, the ass in classics is like front and center.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:45.460
It's definitely, there's definitely some like hidden innuendos there.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:58.740
Joe, you had mentioned, you know, at the closer to the top of this show about the, the blue star and the yellow star that, uh, or asterisks, whatever that select the game mode for you.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:06.660
Mm-hmm. And, you know, do you ever think we'll see the yellow asterisk in like Super Smash Brothers as a character?

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.220
I think it's already an item.

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:15.360
Well, I, I think that the, the yellow asterisk has become, is actually the angry son in, in Super Mario Brothers 3.

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:16.660
I think that's where it ended up.

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:17.900
That makes sense.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:19.600
That, I, that works out for me.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:24.220
And I, I appreciate you adding that to the Super Mario lore.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:27.960
Right. Yeah. I mean, I, I just assumed that everyone kind of like figured.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:30.960
It's never been like explicitly stated, but it's like, look at it.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:32.360
It's an asterisk. It's a yellow asterisk.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:39.680
Now, do you think that there's any significance to how many points are on the asterisk?

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:44.860
Because there's eight. And usually when I see an asterisk, I see maybe six.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:48.860
Is there, is there some numerology happening here?

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:54.180
Like, uh, is this, is this pointing to how many games Donkey Kong will eventually be in?

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:56.340
Uh, or how many they plan?

00:14:56.800 --> 00:15:04.340
Well, you know, the NES is an eight bit system, so they could only, they could only hold up to eight directions on the asterisk.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:05.520
You're right. I forgot.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:07.240
Yeah. That makes sense.

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:10.120
Yeah. Instead of six, which they would have tried to do if they had enough.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:11.560
Mm-hmm. Right.

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:13.180
If they were 16 bit.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:15.320
Right. Then there's a six in the number.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:16.660
So then they can do six.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:28.960
Obviously. Yeah. But another thing about the asterisk I'm actually just noticing now is that it's really just sort of like, if you look at all the two vertical points and the two horizontal points, they're connected to the center.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:33.780
But the diagonal points are just kind of tacked on, almost like they're behind it.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:39.880
So is it even really an asterisk or is it like, yeah, is it like a star and then an X behind it?

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:44.840
I think it's saying that it's actually taking a religious stance.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:48.400
Exactly. Well, you know that guy on YouTube?

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:51.940
That guy on YouTube who like boundary breaks the games?

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:57.480
Yeah. I think we need to do like a Donkey Kong Classic boundary break and see what's behind the asterisk.

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:58.800
How much depth there really is in there.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:10.580
And I bet you, honestly, I bet you like if we start doing that, we'll notice that like the P in player is really small but close to the camera and the L is like gigantic but really far away from the camera.

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:13.040
You know, once you shift it, it's a whole different picture.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:19.780
Well, now that I'm looking at it, and this is the last thing I'm going to say on the asterisk until we can actually get some research.

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:26.600
But if you look at it, if you squint just a little bit, it's actually a picture of a windmill.

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:29.060
Like the back side of a windmill.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:39.020
Oh, yeah. Because, yeah, because you can see the center pole and that square shaped thing in the middle.

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:41.640
That's the building itself.

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:47.760
And then, you know, the diagonal lines, those are the blades of the windmill.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.020
That would make a lot of sense because wasn't there, isn't there like some theory?

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:54.500
Isn't there something where the character of Donkey Kong is based on Don Quixote?

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:56.360
I believe so, yeah.

00:16:56.840 --> 00:17:00.340
Okay, last thing on the asterisk, and then we're moving off of this.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:06.860
But, you know, because it has eight points and everything, do you think that that could just be like the spider's legs?

00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:07.960
And that's a spider?

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:12.260
And maybe that's the spider that like bit Peter Parker and turned him into Spider-Man?

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:19.520
Here's why I don't think that's the case, and that's because most spiders don't have legs that come out of their face.

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:30.900
And whatever, like, direction that this spider may be facing, one of its legs would have to be coming out of its face if that were a spider.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:36.360
You know what I'm saying? Yeah, yeah, that's true, actually, because there's one that's facing directly forward.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:43.700
But if it were true, it would be kind of interesting because, Sean, you keep talking about this unique color palette, red and blue.

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:45.820
That's Spider-Man's color.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:48.140
That's his color. And those are the colors of Spider-Man, yeah.

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:49.260
So, I mean...

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:51.560
Now, I did not say red and blue.

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:53.960
I said, like, an off-red.

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:57.900
It's more of, like, a pinkish red, which is not Spider-Man.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:04.940
It's more a color associated with Crazy Quilt.

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:11.920
So, I think it may be, like, a cross-promotion between DC and Marvel.

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:20.300
Well, that's fair, unless it's kind of more based on the first Sam Raimi film where he's wearing those, like, pajama Spider-Man.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:22.980
I feel like it's closer to those colors than his actual Spider-Man suit.

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:25.060
It's very possible.

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:28.600
I just don't see it, like, in the canon.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:30.300
Mm-hmm. That's fair.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:43.920
Now, had the game not been called Donkey Kong Classics, what would you have called it that you think would maybe fit better within the realm and design constraints of this menu screen?

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:45.540
Spider-Man 4. Yeah, I see.

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:46.660
Like, you could say that.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:47.820
I wouldn't say that.

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:50.360
I would...

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:52.200
But you could. You could say that.

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:55.800
You could put those syllables together and say those words.

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:58.760
Mm-hmm. But I wouldn't. Because here's what I would call it.

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:00.640
I would call it...

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:01.760
I would...

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:12.080
You know, because two games, Donkey Kong and Donkey Kong Jr., I would make a portmanteau of those two games and call it Donkey Kong Jr.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:14.760
Oh! Oh, I like that.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:26.960
Yeah. For anybody keeping score at home, the red in Donkey Kong Classics is actually hashtag D33355.

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:34.800
And the blue is color hashtag 2300B7.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:39.380
I don't know if I'm allowed to legally give out those hexadecimal values.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:41.760
No, no, no. Yeah. We're going to have to cut that.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.400
Yeah. But now you can picture it.

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:48.760
But I think to Joe's point, it would help you picture it better had you have known those hex values.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:50.380
That's true. Yeah. It's...

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:51.980
I do have a...

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:55.760
Everybody can kind of translate hex value to color in their head.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.600
Yeah. That's how seeing works.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:00.700
Here we go.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:02.600
We're finally here.

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:04.720
Performing for you.

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:08.580
If you know the words, you can join in two.

00:20:08.860 --> 00:20:10.420
Put your hands together.

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:12.280
If you want to clap.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.140
As we take you through this funky rap.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:17.900
Hey, P.K.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:20.060
Punky Kong.

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:22.340
He's the leader of the bunch.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.040
You know him well.

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:28.100
He's finally back to kick saw's tail.

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:31.800
His coconut gun can fire in spurts.

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:35.560
If he shoots you, it's gonna hurt.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:39.400
He's bigger, faster, and stronger too.

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:43.000
He's the first member of the D.K.

00:20:43.180 --> 00:20:45.060
crew. Hey, P.K.

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:49.360
You know, Joe, that is a good point that that's what seeing is for.

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:54.180
And, you know, speaking of just seeing the menu, there's also, like, an entire game here.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:55.260
Just beyond the menu.

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:56.560
There's two, actually.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:57.980
You're right. Wait.

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:02.040
There's Donkey Kong and Donkey Kong Jr.

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:03.840
Donkey Kong Jr.

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:07.460
Yes. Now, how do these games hold up for you, Sean?

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:17.400
Um, I mean, they, I remember when we, when we did our very intimate Donkey Kong sessions, just you and I.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:25.880
I can't even remember what our verdict was on how they, on how they, they handle the day.

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:28.500
But they play exactly the same.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:30.080
There's absolutely no difference.

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:43.380
Um, in fact, I remember when we were talking about the omission of one of the Donkey Kong levels, we thought it was just because, you know, there's not enough space on the, the ROM.

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:47.500
Um, but the fact that they could squeeze in Donkey Kong Jr.

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:50.480
on this thing makes me very much doubt that.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:52.580
Yeah, I'm in 100% agreement with you there.

00:21:52.580 --> 00:22:00.900
I'm kind of surprised that they would, uh, bring Donkey Kong exactly the same here, put Donkey Kong Jr.

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:03.920
on it, and still not give us the Pie Factory level.

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:09.580
Yeah. So, I mean, it actually makes me more angry at this game for not being complete.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:14.480
It's funny, too, because I, I always thought that, too, that it's, like, just, it was just a space issue.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:18.420
But then we just think about all of the other, like, hundreds of games we're playing.

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:24.280
They all fit more on it than, like, what seems to exist in these three levels of Donkey Kong.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:27.140
So now that I think about it, why would I have ever thought that?

00:22:27.220 --> 00:22:28.540
Like, why did I ever think that?

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:30.600
Well, you know why?

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:34.420
Because it, it, it would, you're putting good faith in Nintendo here.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:43.900
But they don't care because they made this, they released this version of Donkey Kong back in 1983 as the very first game for the Famicom.

00:22:44.120 --> 00:22:45.400
And Donkey Kong Jr.

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:48.300
was the other launch title for the Famicom.

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:54.860
So when you have two launch title Famicom games, you bring them over to North America and you change nothing about them.

00:22:54.980 --> 00:23:01.560
And then you re-release them together and say, okay, but we're still going to leave them exactly the same.

00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:06.220
The only person you're helping there is the person who got screwed over the first time when they bought Donkey Kong.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.940
So they don't feel inclined to buy this classic version.

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:18.580
Yeah. The fact that there is nothing added makes me wonder, like, what the point was.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:28.420
If it's just, if it's the equivalent of games going on sale and you get two for one because they're so old now, maybe.

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:33.240
But I guess I don't really know aside from maybe this being bundled.

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:38.160
Yeah. I think of bundling it as, like, kind of the best way that I can think of it.

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:44.140
I mean, if you think about it, I'm sure, like, everybody that you've ever met has once owned Super Mario Duck Hunt.

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:46.740
Like, right? Like, everybody had that somewhere.

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:47.980
Super Mario slash Duck Hunt.

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:50.240
And I just felt like that was just such a normal thing.

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:52.960
I was like, yeah, it made sense.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:54.020
Even though I never really thought about it.

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:56.160
It made sense. Yeah. You just put two games together that are really popular.

00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:00.860
So maybe it was just like they knew people would buy this bundled together, you know?

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:07.900
I think it's a smart move to bundle the games, especially something like Donkey Kong, which was huge in the arcades when it came out.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:13.780
Another thing that we're not really considering, and I'm going to continue to seesaw my opinion on this as I go on.

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:21.860
But Donkey Kong is the big Nintendo name right now in terms of, like, mascot characters, right?

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:25.680
Super Mario Brothers took off in 1985 and everything.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:31.560
But Donkey Kong is, like, what really started Nintendo on this hot track of video games.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:38.980
And it's only once, like, Mario continues to have success with Super Mario Brothers and Super Mario Brothers 3 and Super Mario World.

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:41.400
Like, you know, he becomes the face.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:44.820
But Donkey Kong is, like, the start of everything.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:47.300
And Mario is just a character in that universe.

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:55.560
Now it's almost like this release of Donkey Kong Classics is supposed to usher in, like, the, oh, but don't forget about our mascot, Donkey Kong.

00:24:55.720 --> 00:24:58.660
Like, we want everyone, you know, brand awareness and everything.

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:00.160
And that makes sense.

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:05.780
But then they go on to never release another Donkey Kong game until Donkey Kong 94.

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:12.020
Yeah, this seems to be the last gasp of Donkey Kong as the mascot.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:20.440
But beyond that, they're not doing, they're not putting their all into saving him.

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:22.460
You know? You'd think that they would make a better game.

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:29.960
Yeah, it almost feels like, right, it feels like you would want this Donkey Kong Classics, you would want, like, Super Mario Classics,

00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:36.700
in which we get, like, Super Mario Brothers and Super Mario Brothers 2, the Japan one, on the same cart.

00:25:36.760 --> 00:25:41.820
And that's how you, like, alleviate the stress of buying, like, Super Mario Brothers 2 Japan.

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:49.480
You know, instead we get Super Mario Brothers 2 here later on this year and what would be referred to as Super Mario Brothers USA moving forward.

00:25:49.480 --> 00:26:00.040
But I feel like that would have been an appropriate way to introduce the Japanese version of Super Mario Brothers 2 to this American audience is by bundling it with Super Mario Brothers 1.

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:04.560
It's this way it's like you get the game you love plus a brand new version of that same game.

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:09.020
Whereas Donkey Kong Classics isn't really offering any kind of, like, incentive.

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:10.540
Yeah, there's no additional value here.

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:14.400
Yeah. If you already own the games, there's no reason to buy this.

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:21.020
So, Joe, you weren't on the Donkey Kong episode, but how do you feel about, like, Donkey Kong and Donkey Kong Jr.'s video games?

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:30.200
I mean, on one level, as, like, you know, as playing it on an NES and it being very obvious that this is an older arcade game,

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:32.240
I think that shows to me a lot.

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:41.140
This doesn't feel like if I had no nostalgia for Donkey Kong in the arcade, I don't think I'd be that interested after playing it on the NES.

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:51.240
But then even, I think, as an arcade game, just my personal preference, I've always thought it was a little more popular than it is good, at least in my opinion.

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:54.900
I mean, it's fine. I know you guys really liked it a lot on the episode, if I remember correctly.

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:56.840
I think it's fine.

00:26:56.860 --> 00:26:58.080
It's fun. It's a game.

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.380
It operates. Historically, it's very important.

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:05.500
But I just always think that, like, it doesn't stand out to me.

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:08.480
Got it. I think I'll say that Donkey Kong Jr.

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:12.120
is not getting better as I continue to play more video games.

00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:19.080
But Donkey Kong has always had, like, a soft spot for me, and maybe it's just because I played it early on in my life that I still enjoy it.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:23.880
However, I do have one very specific nitpick about Donkey Kong here.

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:38.640
And that is that they're in the NES version specifically, not the arcade, there just seems to be a, like, muscle memory error in me that causes me to miscount the number of climbs on a ladder.

00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:48.100
And I always seem to, like, just be one too short before I, like, am able to move left or right before I'm actually able to.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:49.820
Yeah, I did the exact same thing.

00:27:49.820 --> 00:27:59.000
I was just stuck on that, just hovering above the ladder, acting like I was still on it, and a barrel was coming right from my face.

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:01.300
I'm glad I'm not the only one.

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:13.800
Just to give you a little development thing here, in an early issue of Nintendo Power, they referenced the return of Donkey Kong, which is the name of the video game.

00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:15.480
And there's, like, a little teaser about it.

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:16.780
We'll link to it in the show notes.

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:20.740
This is not that return of Donkey Kong game.

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:22.860
Or is it?

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:33.600
See, I would argue that this is the return of Donkey Kong game, and that they were never working on, like, a Donkey Kong 4 or a new version of Donkey Kong.

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:38.240
And that the return of Donkey Kong was just teasing the Donkey Kong classics thing.

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:44.320
And maybe Nintendo of America, who works on Nintendo Power, just didn't get the memo that this is what it was going to become.

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:59.560
But I do think hyping up a return of Donkey Kong and not getting the Donkey Kong game I pitched back on the Donkey Kong 3 episode, which is finally being able to control Donkey Kong and fight Mario that way, which would have been an amazing game.

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:04.900
To never get that, and to have this instead be the return of Donkey Kong, it hurts.

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:17.720
No, I was just going to say, I think, like, for us looking at it now, or at least for me looking at it now, it seems so much less of a big deal because it's like, yeah, a bunch of these things exist from the past.

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:28.980
But, you know, if you do think about it in the lens of, like, you are a Donkey Kong fan in the 80s and you're waiting for this new game to come out and then the new game is just the old game.

00:29:28.980 --> 00:29:33.440
I can see why that is, like, a much bigger deal than, like, now just being like, oh, why is this bundled?

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:43.560
Well, I mean, I also don't think that, as much as we've kind of messed around, I don't think that anyone was fooled by this.

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:50.140
I don't think that there was an announcement and lots of anticipation about these two games on the same cart.

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:56.260
Right. But it just seems odd that this would be the business decision.

00:29:56.260 --> 00:30:00.100
Unless it was bundled with the console.

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:07.040
Yeah, feels like you would want to at least include Donkey Kong 3, but they were smart enough not to include that, thankfully, because I hate that game.

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:52.940
Yeah. Hey, yo!

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:54.720
Look out, down below

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:57.200
Here he comes, Banana Slammer

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:58.780
Come on, go, hero

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:00.280
Hey-oh

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:02.580
Donkey Kong, let's go, let's go

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:04.680
Here he comes, Banana Slammer

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:10.220
Anyway, you know, this was just making me think about something that's pretty

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:16.560
It's having a moment in the video game industry since at least the tail end of the

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:22.320
or maybe the, sorry, the beginning of the PS4, Xbox One generation of consoles

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:30.200
and that's that whole like remake, remaster, re-release trend of video games that we always see

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:34.380
Where do you guys like find yourself in the mix of all these things?

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:35.600
Are remakes okay?

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.420
Are remasters preferred?

00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:41.200
Do you even pick up games that are just straight re-releases?

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:46.280
I'm thinking about like that Mafia trilogy that just got announced and was released like simultaneously

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:52.560
and that's like the weird mix of like one game is a straight up re-release

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:55.680
the other one's a remaster and the first one's a remake

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:57.240
like that is super strange, right?

00:31:57.300 --> 00:31:58.800
We never really see something like that

00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:00.660
Yeah, but I think it's super interesting

00:32:00.660 --> 00:32:05.000
and I'm not for or against one over the other

00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:12.660
I think that there are a lot of remakes that have done good by its source material

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:17.340
There are a lot that have completely just wasted it

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:22.080
Like I remember there was a game from the 90s, Syndicate

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:26.860
which was like a, not tactics, it was like a squad-based shooter

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:32.700
And then Syndicate comes out on like PS3, Xbox 360 generation

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:36.380
and it's just another FPS game

00:32:36.380 --> 00:32:39.880
So that was a remake that nobody asked for and nobody liked

00:32:41.500 --> 00:32:46.080
But you can also ruin remasters

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:50.000
Like when Silent Hill 2 and 3 were released together

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:54.600
that sounded like the perfect thing for me to buy on the newer systems

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:58.420
but they actually mess those games up

00:32:58.420 --> 00:33:01.920
They are objectively worse than their original

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:08.420
So I think like it really depends on why the games are being remastered

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:14.280
Is it because the developers think that they deserve better fidelity

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:16.400
or is it because they're trying to make a quick buck?

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:22.440
So I kind of have a muddled opinion on them all

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:24.300
Yeah, got it

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:28.240
And Joe, I just would love to hear before you dive deeper

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:30.500
I'd love to hear your thoughts on just like

00:33:30.500 --> 00:33:32.940
what counts as a re-release for you

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:35.140
what counts as a remaster and what counts as a remake

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:37.520
Well, so the way I always looked at it

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:39.620
was that a remaster is

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:42.220
is if something is taken

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:45.600
the original files, the original game is taken

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:48.340
and maybe up-resed and maybe a few little like

00:33:48.340 --> 00:33:50.200
interface details are added

00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:53.400
and it's made compatible with like the current generation

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.240
That could not be the actual definition

00:33:55.240 --> 00:33:56.880
but that's what I always assumed

00:33:57.580 --> 00:33:58.840
I would agree with that

00:33:58.840 --> 00:34:00.120
Whereas a remake is like

00:34:00.860 --> 00:34:03.140
either taking the original game

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:05.200
and using it as your roadmap

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:06.840
to make the game again

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:08.680
now with the current technology

00:34:08.680 --> 00:34:11.180
like the Crash Bandicoot Insane Trilogy

00:34:11.180 --> 00:34:13.680
which is literally just the original games

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:15.840
now with better graphics, a little better controls

00:34:15.840 --> 00:34:18.480
and, you know, a few other little things added to it

00:34:18.480 --> 00:34:20.660
but it's like clearly a game that was made from scratch

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:24.240
or something like the Ratchet & Clank

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:26.940
